  },
  "locales": {
    "en": {
      "title": "Concrete and abstract nouns",
      "summary": "A concrete noun names what we can touch; an abstract noun names an idea or quality.",
      "status": "draft",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "What it is",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraphs",
              "paragraphs": [
                "A concrete noun names something you can see or touch. An abstract noun names an idea, a quality or a feeling."
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "**table**, **dog**, **bread** (concrete)",
                "**freedom**, **idea**, **fear** (abstract)",
                "**kindness** comes from the adjective **kind**."
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "How it works in English",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraphs",
              "paragraphs": [
                "Many abstract nouns are made from adjectives or verbs with endings such as -ness, -ity, -ment and -tion."
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "html": "<table><thead><tr><th>Base word</th><th>Abstract noun</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>happy</strong></td><td><strong>happiness</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>able</strong></td><td><strong>ability</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>move</strong></td><td><strong>movement</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>inform</strong></td><td><strong>information</strong></td></tr></tbody></table>"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}
